5
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:49.970
aynı günün `felsefe.txt` bölümünün sadık İngilizce çevirisini veriyorum — ki bu çeviri bugün 05:27'de `ceviri_uret.py` tarafından zaten `philosophy.txt` olarak üretilmiş durumda.

6
00:00:49.970 --> 00:00:56.500
Aşağıdaki metin o dosyanın gövdesidir (kanca, jingle ve kapanış çıkarılmış hâli).

7
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.350
Picture a tent in the desert.

8
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:05.880
The sun is going down, the sand is beginning to cool, and a single shadow appears on the horizon.

9
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.570
Someone is walking.

10
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.780
You don't know who they are.

11
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:17.470
What tribe they come from, whether they're a friend or an enemy, a debtor or a fugitive — you have no idea.

12
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.560
The only thing you know is this: if they spend the night out there, they will die.

13
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.730
The water isn't theirs, it's yours.

14
00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.900
The shade isn't theirs, it's yours.

15
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.150
Whether this man lives or dies comes down to a decision you will make in the next few minutes.

16
00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:41.480
The oldest moral question in human history was born, I suspect, right there, at the mouth of that tent.

17
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.090
It is a question that comes before even the prohibition on killing.

18
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:54.700
Because not killing requires you to do nothing; taking in a stranger, on the other hand, requires you to share the little you have with someone you don't know.

19
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.030
The first is holding your hand back.

20
00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.880
The second is opening it.

21
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.450
Desert societies answered this question not with custom, but with something close to law.

22
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:11.220
Taking in a stranger wasn't courtesy, it was obligation.

23
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:17.110
To refuse was shame, and in that part of the world, shame was a lethal currency.

24
00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:30.040
When Ibn Khaldun describes bedouin life in the Muqaddimah, he sees that this is not romantic but structural: with no state, no court, no police force, what holds people up is the bond of solidarity between them.

25
00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.370
Generosity is the visible face of that bond.

26
00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:38.300
The one who gives water today knows that tomorrow he will be the one going thirsty.

27
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:44.190
Hospitality is insurance, invented in a world where insurance had not yet been invented.

28
00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.720
Today you, tomorrow me.

29
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:52.130
But if that were the whole story, hospitality would have had no business with the gods.

30
00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.020
And yet it did.

31
00:02:54.020 --> 00:03:01.350
In ancient Greece there was a separate epithet for the protector of strangers: Zeus Xenios, Zeus of guests.

32
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:06.920
A man who mistreated a stranger was trampling not on a rule of etiquette, but on the god himself.

33
00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.730
In Homer's Odyssey, this is the hidden measure of the story.

34
00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:19.180
Every time the hero washes up on an island, the same test is set: will this door open, and if it does, how will it open?

35
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.990
In the land of the Phaeacians, it opens.

36
00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:28.400
The man has come out of the sea, caked in salt, naked, without name or reputation.

37
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.570
They bathe him, they clothe him, they feed him.

38
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.220
Only after all of that do they ask his name.

39
00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:42.070
Notice the order, because the whole point lies in that order: hospitality first, identity second.

40
00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:47.800
To take someone in before learning who they are is to sever hospitality from identity altogether.

41
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:51.930
The very same epic contains the exact inverse of this rule.

42
00:03:51.930 --> 00:04:00.540
The Cyclops Polyphemus tells the guests who arrive at his cave that he will give them a guest-gift, and announces that his gift will be to eat them last.

43
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:07.390
For a Greek audience, this was more than a scene of monstrosity: it was a description of stepping outside of humanity.

44
00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:13.760
The one-eyed giant was strong, he had his cave, he had his flock, he needed nothing from anyone.

45
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.210
And precisely for that reason, he was incapable of hospitality.

46
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:23.620
The same threshold is set, in an entirely different key, at Abraham's tent.

47
00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.390
As it is told in the Quran, guests he does not recognize come to him.

48
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:33.800
He quietly slips away to his household, roasts a fattened calf and sets it before them.

49
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:39.210
When the guests do not reach out for the food, a fear settles into him.

50
00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:46.860
The detail here is very fine: the man is not afraid of his guests, he is afraid that the guest-relation has broken down.

51
00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:53.390
A guest who will not sit at your table is, in the language of this tradition, a sign of threat.

52
00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.840
To share food is to declare that there is no longer enmity between you.

53
00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:06.450
The Islamic tradition would later bind this attitude with a very clear sentence: whoever believes in God and the Last Day, let him honor his guest.

54
00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.300
Look at the structure of that sentence.

55
00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:17.270
Hospitality is established here not as a habit, a temperament, a personality trait, but as the direct consequence of faith.

56
00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:21.720
Failing to be hospitable becomes not rudeness, but inconsistency.

57
00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:31.130
So three separate traditions, independently of one another, arrive at the same place: the stranger at the door is not an ordinary person.

58
00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.500
There is an extra weight to them.

59
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:39.550
The Greek calls that weight the god himself, because he thinks the one knocking might be a deity in disguise.

60
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.400
The Semitic tradition calls it an angel.

61
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.530
The bedouin gives it no name at all, he simply knows.

62
00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:50.500
So where does that weight come from?

63
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.390
From prudence?

64
00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:56.680
From the calculation that tomorrow I too might be stranded on the road?

65
00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:04.970
Or is there something else beneath all of this, something no calculation explains, something that does not disappear even when the need is gone?

66
00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.140
Because we don't go thirsty anymore.

67
00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:11.790
We don't spend our nights on caravan routes.

68
00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.760
We have insurance, we have courts, we have hotels.

69
00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.250
The reciprocity equation was solved long ago.

70
00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:24.340
And yet, when there's a knock at the door, something still stirs inside us.

71
00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:29.110
In the twentieth century, one thinker tried to name that stirring thing exactly.

72
00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:35.800
And in doing so, he took hospitality out of the realm of custom and made it the most fundamental question in philosophy.

73
00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:43.130
Emmanuel Levinas was born in nineteen oh six in Kaunas, a city that lies today within the borders of Lithuania.

74
00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:50.300
He studied philosophy in France, attended the lectures of Husserl and Heidegger in Germany, and became a French citizen.

75
00:06:50.300 --> 00:07:00.990
In nineteen thirty-nine he was called up for military service; in nineteen forty he was taken prisoner, and he spent the rest of the war in a prison camp in Germany, working in forestry.

76
00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:07.360
Because he wore a French uniform, he was classed as a prisoner of war, and he survived.

77
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.130
Of the family he had left behind in Lithuania, no one remained.

78
00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:16.260
His mother, his father and his two brothers were murdered.

79
00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:21.190
His wife and his young daughter survived, hidden in a monastery in France.

80
00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:27.080
Years after those years in the camp, in a short piece of writing, he recalls a strange memory.

81
00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:34.090
Every day the team of prisoners was marched out to work under guard, and the people they passed along the way would not look at them.

82
00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:41.420
Not looking is the most refined form of violence; they were not being treated as human beings, they were seen as talking animals.

83
00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:44.590
Then a stray dog appears around the edge of the camp.

84
00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:49.840
For a few weeks he greets them every morning, wagging his tail, barking with delight.

85
00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:58.610
Levinas names him Bobby and says that this dog was the only being who saw them: the only being who never doubted for a moment that the prisoners were human.

86
00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:05.940
The whole of Levinas's philosophy is, in a sense, an accounting of how that kind of look can go missing among human beings.

87
00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:14.550
In his great book of nineteen sixty-one, Totality and Infinity, he argues that philosophy has been starting from the wrong question for centuries.

88
00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:20.760
Western thought has always asked about being: what is it, what is it made of, how can it be known.

89
00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.090
Levinas says this is not the first question.

90
00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:30.300
The first question is this: who is the person in front of me, and what do I owe them?

91
00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:35.230
Ethics is not a branch of philosophy; it is where philosophy begins.

92
00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:40.000
And he gives a name to the place where that beginning happens: the face.

93
00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.330
For Levinas, the face is not an image.

94
00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:47.940
It is not the length of a nose, the colour of an eye, the shape of a jaw.

95
00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:51.910
It is certainly not the photograph on an identity card.

96
00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:56.680
The face is the fact that a person will never fit inside all your ideas about them.

97
00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:02.410
You define them, you classify them, you put them in a box, and they overflow the box.

98
00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:05.420
The face is what overflows.

99
00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:09.710
And according to Levinas, the face speaks.

100
00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:13.360
Without uttering a single word, it says something.

101
00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.810
What it says is this: you shall not kill me.

102
00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.820
You shall not turn me into an instrument.

103
00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:26.550
This is not a plea, it is a command; and yet there is no power standing behind it.

104
00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:30.360
The strength of the face is precisely its powerlessness.

105
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:36.730
The person before you is utterly defenceless, and what binds you is precisely that defencelessness.

106
00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:41.340
To see how strange an idea this is, you need only look at everyday life.

107
00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.510
You are walking down a crowded street.

108
00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.640
Someone is sitting at the edge of the pavement with a cup in their hand.

109
00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.210
What do you do?

110
00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:54.180
You do what most of us do: you don't look.

111
00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:59.430
You look at your phone, you look at a shop window, you look across the road.

112
00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.120
Where does that reflex come from?

113
00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:04.970
Not from stinginess.

114
00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:08.300
The money in your pocket isn't really the point.

115
00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.430
You don't look, because the moment you do, something begins.

116
00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:18.960
Meeting someone's eyes opens a contract, and you have no idea what that contract is going to cost you.

117
00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:26.610
Levinas's entire claim is hidden in that small, evasive glance: to see the face is already to bring the debt into being.

118
00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.300
Which is why we choose not to see.

119
00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:34.550
Levinas insists on one particular feature of this debt: it is not equal.

120
00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:37.560
It is not reciprocal.

121
00:10:37.560 --> 00:10:42.650
I owe the other person more than they owe me, and this asymmetry cannot be corrected.

122
00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:45.020
It is nothing like an exchange.

123
00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:48.190
It is not a matter of you give, and then I'll give.

124
00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:55.520
Because the moment I begin calculating what will come back to me, the other stops being a face and turns into a line in a ledger.

125
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:59.170
It is worth remembering who the man saying this was.

126
00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:02.500
A man whose family had been slaughtered.

127
00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:14.310
His second great book, published in nineteen seventy-four, he dedicated to the memory of the six million people murdered by the National Socialists, and to all the other human beings who were victims of the same hatred,

128
00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.000
the same hatred of the foreigner.

129
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.770
The last part of that dedication matters enormously: all the others.

130
00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:27.180
Even while counting his own dead, he keeps taking in those who fall outside the count.

131
00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:33.070
Because what Levinas saw was this: genocide begins with the erasure of the face.

132
00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:41.520
You turn people into a category, you count what you have turned into a category, you administer what you have counted, and you eliminate what you administer.

133
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:47.410
The first link in that whole chain is forged by a gesture that looks perfectly innocent: not looking.

134
00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:50.580
Up to this point everything seems clear.

135
00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.990
But it is exactly here that the most uncomfortable question in philosophy comes into play.

136
00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:59.000
Granted: the face binds me.

137
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.050
But how far?

138
00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.740
All the way?

139
00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:07.390
My house, my bread, my child's room as well?

140
00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:10.240
Am I not entitled to stop somewhere?

141
00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:14.370
And if I do stop, what is the name of the place where I stopped?

142
00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:18.820
Tradition had a very practical answer to that question: three days.

143
00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:22.630
The wording in the hadith sources is unambiguous.

144
00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:28.360
The feast lasts three days; anything beyond that is charity on the part of the host.

145
00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:33.130
In other words, hospitality is an obligation, but it has a limit.

146
00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:38.700
After three days the hosting may continue, yet it ceases to be a debt and becomes a gift.

147
00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:41.870
That number did not stay confined to books.

148
00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:46.480
The caravanserais of Anatolia were built to the very same measure.

149
00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:58.450
The arrangement described in the endowment charters of the Seljuk period runs like this: a traveller arrives, and for three days both he and his animal are lodged free of charge; if he falls ill he is cared for;

150
00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:06.260
if his shoes are torn they are mended; and for none of this does anyone need to ask about his religion, his language, or where he has come from.

151
00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:11.510
A Muslim merchant and an Armenian merchant sleep in the same courtyard with the same right.

152
00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:15.640
Then the third morning comes, and the road goes on.

153
00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.450
One has to admit this is an ingenious solution.

154
00:13:19.450 --> 00:13:23.900
A boundless obligation is converted into a bearable one.

155
00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.750
But it is also an unsettling solution.

156
00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:32.480
Because it implies something: if you are still there on the fourth day, you are no longer a guest.

157
00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.850
So what are you?

158
00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:43.140
In a short treatise called Perpetual Peace, written in seventeen ninety-five, Immanuel Kant tried to draw precisely this line.

159
00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:50.150
Setting out the conditions for a lasting peace among states, he devoted the third and final article to hospitality.

160
00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:57.320
What he said was this: every human being has the right not to be treated as an enemy on foreign soil where he sets foot.

161
00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:00.490
Notice that this is not an appeal to charity.

162
00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:03.660
Kant says right, explicitly.

163
00:14:03.660 --> 00:14:08.110
And he grounds that right in a reason: the earth is a sphere.

164
00:14:08.110 --> 00:14:11.440
We cannot move away from one another forever.

165
00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:16.530
On a round planet, sooner or later everyone comes up against everyone else's border.

166
00:14:16.530 --> 00:14:21.300
Common ownership of the land is not a sentiment but a geometrical necessity.

167
00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:26.870
Yet immediately afterwards Kant makes the sharp distinction we are still arguing about today.

168
00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:30.680
This is a right of visit, not a right of residence.

169
00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:35.130
The one who arrives cannot demand to live there simply because he has arrived.

170
00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:38.460
The host country may turn him away.

171
00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:43.230
On a single condition: that turning him away will not lead to his destruction.

172
00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:47.840
It also matters that Kant says whom he had in mind while writing these lines.

173
00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:51.330
He was thinking of the European states of his own age.

174
00:14:51.330 --> 00:15:00.260
What countries that passed for civilized were doing in the Americas, in Africa, in Asia, he described as the exact opposite of hospitality.

175
00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:03.430
They had not visited; they had conquered.

176
00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:15.080
So even at the moment of its birth, the principle of hospitality faced two ways: while it protected the one knocking at the door, it also passed judgment on the powerful one who broke the door down and walked in.

177
00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:21.290
Two centuries later Jacques Derrida went after this orderly boundary Kant had drawn, and found a crack in it.

178
00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:27.020
In his lectures on hospitality, he showed that the word itself carries a civil war inside it.

179
00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:31.630
In Latin, the word for guest and the word for enemy stem from the same root.

180
00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:36.400
The man at the door is at once the one to be welcomed and the one who might be a threat.

181
00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:45.330
Even in our own language this ambivalence lies hidden: the word foreigner names both the one who comes from far away and the one who does not belong inside.

182
00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.180
The impasse Derrida saw is this.

183
00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:52.310
True, pure hospitality would have to be unconditional.

184
00:15:52.310 --> 00:16:01.080
It would mean saying yes to whoever comes, without asking their name, without demanding their papers, without calculating how long they will stay.

185
00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:06.330
The instant you ask a single question, hospitality ends and selection begins.

186
00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:10.300
But this unconditional yes is at the same time impossible.

187
00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:14.590
Because a house open to everyone forever is no longer a house.

188
00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:19.520
And once it is not a house, there is no place left in which to welcome anyone.

189
00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:25.090
So the very thing that makes hospitality possible is the thing that ruins it: the door.

190
00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:29.700
Without a door you cannot invite anyone in; with a door you can never open fully.

191
00:16:29.700 --> 00:16:33.670
It is very easy to draw two wrong conclusions from this.

192
00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:38.920
The first: since pure hospitality is impossible, the whole thing is empty.

193
00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:44.650
The second: since we are obliged to set conditions, every condition we set is legitimate.

194
00:16:44.650 --> 00:16:47.660
Neither of these is what Derrida said.

195
00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:54.510
What he said is this: our conditional rules must be perpetually disturbed by that impossible unconditionality.

196
00:16:54.510 --> 00:17:02.960
The three-day rule is a good rule, but the state of the person left outside the door on the evening of the third day must keep putting that rule back on trial.

197
00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:07.570
Law is necessary; a law satisfied with itself is dangerous.

198
00:17:07.570 --> 00:17:11.380
And then there is the reversal Derrida was fond of.

199
00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:17.110
The one who opens the door, in the moment of opening it, stops being free in his own house.

200
00:17:17.110 --> 00:17:24.120
Once the guest arrives, you can no longer decide alone when you will sleep, what you will eat, which room you will use.

201
00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:27.450
The host becomes a kind of hostage to his guest.

202
00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:30.620
To open is to hand over part of your control.

203
00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:36.670
And it is this handing over, not the expense, that makes opening the door so hard.

204
00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:41.760
And today that handing over is being debated on a scale never seen in history.

205
00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:45.090
Because there is no longer a single traveller at the door.

206
00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:54.340
According to figures from the United Nations refugee agency, by the middle of twenty twenty-five the number of people driven from their homes worldwide by war,

207
00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:58.470
persecution and violence had passed one hundred and twenty-two million.

208
00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:02.280
That is one out of every sixty-seven people on the planet.

209
00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:05.610
Turkey stood squarely at the centre of this picture.

210
00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:16.300
At the end of twenty twenty the number of Syrians under temporary protection had reached three million six hundred and forty-one thousand; by the middle of twenty twenty-five, with returns,

211
00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:20.910
that figure had fallen to around two million seven hundred thousand.

212
00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:26.160
For more than a decade this was the country hosting more refugees than any other in the world.

213
00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:33.330
Something very strange happens when we say those numbers out loud, and that strangeness is what this episode is really about.

214
00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:36.820
As the figures grow, the face disappears.

215
00:18:36.820 --> 00:18:39.670
Three million is not a face.

216
00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:43.160
One hundred twenty-two million certainly isn't.

217
00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:51.770
The binding claim Levinas spoke of was born in the gaze of a single human being; politics, meanwhile, has no choice but to work in millions.

218
00:18:51.770 --> 00:19:00.380
And this is precisely where the knot of the modern problem of hospitality lies: the language of ethics is singular, the language of governance is plural.

219
00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.870
The two struggle to translate each other.

220
00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:07.520
And then a screen is added to all this.

221
00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:13.730
In Homer's world, the stranger could only arrive on foot, and when he arrived, he stood before you.

222
00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:17.380
Today the stranger appears on your phone over breakfast.

223
00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:22.790
You see the face of a child pulled from the rubble before you've even decided what you'll have for dinner.

224
00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:26.280
This is an entirely new condition in human history.

225
00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:31.530
And it raises this question: is that thing on the screen a face, or is it an image?

226
00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:37.100
If we stay faithful to Levinas's distinction, the answer is uncomfortable.

227
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:42.990
A face is something that can answer you, that can call you back, that is in the same room as you.

228
00:19:42.990 --> 00:19:48.080
The one on the screen can ask nothing of you, because a flick of your finger makes it vanish.

229
00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.930
You swipe, and it's gone.

230
00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:57.940
The power of the face lay in its defenselessness; the screen shows that defenselessness but cannot deliver its command.

231
00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:05.270
And so we learn something odd: seeing hundreds of instances of suffering every day does not make a person more sensitive.

232
00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:11.960
On the contrary, it produces a mind that grows accustomed to being unable to do anything about what it sees.

233
00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:15.930
This habit has a name — it's called compassion fatigue.

234
00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:19.740
And it isn't heartlessness; it's an overload of capacity.

235
00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:23.870
Then we come to the one nearest to us.

236
00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:31.520
The hardest test of hospitality, contrary to what people assume, isn't given at the border but on the landing outside your door.

237
00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:40.450
In the Islamic tradition there is a striking saying passed down about the neighbour: Gabriel counselled me so often concerning the neighbour that I thought he would make him an heir.

238
00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:43.460
To be an heir means you are bound by blood.

239
00:20:43.460 --> 00:20:51.430
In other words, that person you don't know on the other side of the wall is drawn close to family — not in law, perhaps, but in ethics.

240
00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:56.840
Now ask yourself honestly: do you know the name of the neighbour who steps onto your floor?

241
00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:59.530
Do you know how many people live with them?

242
00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:03.180
Did you hear that they were in hospital last month?

243
00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:11.150
In a country that has taken in millions of people, it is entirely ordinary to nod to someone in the same building for years and never once meet them.

244
00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:15.440
This isn't a contradiction; it's really two ends of the same matter.

245
00:21:15.440 --> 00:21:19.410
Because opening the door is not a feeling, it's a habit.

246
00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:24.020
And habit is formed at the nearest threshold, or it isn't formed at all.

247
00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:32.790
Looking back today, we can see that all those old traditions shared a single insight: they did not leave hospitality to the good intentions of individuals.

248
00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:35.320
They institutionalised it.

249
00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:41.850
They tied the caravanserai to an endowment, the public kitchen to a revenue stream, the three days to a rule.

250
00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:46.300
Because good intentions grow tired; rules do not.

251
00:21:46.300 --> 00:21:54.750
What falls to us is to build the modern equivalent of this: to anchor the welcoming of others not to a mood, but to a system that works.

252
00:21:54.750 --> 00:22:00.640
But a system alone is not enough, because the real decision always remains singular.

253
00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:07.650
In Konya, at the entrance to Rumi's tomb, there are famous lines hanging: whatever you are, come, come again.

254
00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:13.700
Most scholars say these lines belong not to Rumi but to Abu Sa'id, who lived before him.

255
00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:21.510
Who wrote the words may be disputed, but what they say is clear: the invitation is extended before anyone asks what the newcomer is.

256
00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:28.200
Identity comes afterwards — just as it did with that stranger washed and fed on the seashore.

257
00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:33.770
Let's return to the question we asked at the beginning of this episode, and alter it slightly.

258
00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:39.340
To the question of how far you keep your door open, there is no answer in the form of a number.

259
00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:45.870
Three days is an answer; a right of visit is an answer; never opening the door at all is also an answer.

260
00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:51.600
The real issue is not how far that distance extends, but which direction you're facing.

261
00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:56.690
Do you open the door after you've learned who has come, or do you learn after you've opened it?

262
00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:58.900
Which is your order?

263
00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:04.470
Because a door that opens according to identity is not a door, it's a checkpoint.

264
00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:09.880
And a door left open all the way is not a door either, it's a hole in the wall.

265
00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:17.050
A door is a door because it can be opened and closed; and precisely for that reason, it asks you to decide again each time.

266
00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:21.180
Out there in the desert, the shadow on the horizon is still walking.

267
00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:23.230
You are still at the door.

268
00:23:23.230 --> 00:23:28.160
And who the water in your hand belongs to still depends on the answer you will give.

269
00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:31.810
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

270
00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:34.980
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
